(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Objective: To assess the efficacy of direct current brain stimulation versus sham control) Tj
T*
(in reducing alcohol consumption in adults with established alcohol use disorder. Setting:) Tj
T*
(14 outpatient clinical sites in France and Monaco from 2015-2022. Participants: 337) Tj
T*
(randomized adults with alcohol use disorder \(predominantly male, mean age 51 years\).) Tj
T*
(Intervention: 2-mA direct current applied to prefrontal regions daily for 5 days versus) Tj
T*
(sham. Results: At 24 weeks, the active stimulation group showed a statistically) Tj
T*
(significant decrease in days per month with heavy drinking episodes \(mean reduction 2.5) Tj
T*
(days per 4-week period, 97.5% CI: -4.9 to -0.1, p=0.022\) compared to sham. However, total) Tj
T*
(daily alcohol volume reduction was not statistically significant between groups \(-6 g/day,) Tj
T*
(97.5% CI: -15.2 to 3.3, p=0.15\). The intervention was tolerated well, with similar adverse) Tj
T*
(event rates between groups. Conclusions: Active stimulation demonstrated modest, sustained) Tj
T*
(benefit for reducing drinking frequency but not absolute consumption volume.) Tj
